body:
How can I set `serializable`? What do you mean by that?

And is the way I add new comment correct?

``` PHP
$article -> comments = array_merge( (array)$article -> comments, (array)$newComment );
```

Also: do you **recommend any tool to simulate** multiple requests so I can test this at development?
And also one thing. Consider the code above (adding a commnet) and two users, each executing the same code. This will happne in different threads but the computer picks up this order of instructions:

``` PHP
// User 1
$newComment = $this -> Articles -> Comments -> newEntity( $data );
$article = $this -> Articles -> get( $id, [ 'contain' => [ 'Comments' ] ]);

// User 2
$newComment = $this -> Articles -> Comments -> newEntity( $data );
$article = $this -> Articles -> get( $id, [ 'contain' => [ 'Comments' ] ]);

// User 1
$article -> comments = array_merge( (array)$article -> comments, (array)$newComment );
$this -> Articles -> save( $article );

// User 2
$article -> comments = array_merge( (array)$article -> comments, (array)$newComment );
$this -> Articles -> save( $article );
```

So you see: both users firstly get the _Article_ with all _commets_. And first user add's one comment and save. And second users also adds it's own comment and save. But they both added the comment to the _original_ list of comments. So would both comments save? Or could there be a problem that the comment from first user will be deleted, since the second user is saving the _Article_ without knowing the first comment.

And to that issue: So firstly you do one query to save the record and then second query to get the ID, right? And the problem is that this doesn't happen in one transaction, or is not issolated. Because I am using default settings and as I can see, the `save` method is _atomic_ by default so I thought it is ok.

**What method do you use in Postgres?** For me this is _critical bug_, because a lot of people are already using this and makes a problem. Currently the only solution is not to use _save association_ but only do it like:

``` PHP
$newComment -> article_id = $id;
$this -> Articles -> Comments -> save( $newComment );
```

As I've found so far, in **MSSQL** it should be solved using similar approach as in Postgres using `OUTPUT`:

``` SQL
INSERT INTO table (name)
OUTPUT Inserted.ID
VALUES('bob');
```

Could you please help me to implement that? Where is the method (and what SQL command is used) to retrieve the ID value?

I see that in `PostgresDialectTrait` you use something this:

``` PHP
protected function _insertQueryTranslator($query) {
        if (!$query->clause('epilog')) {
            $query->epilog('RETURNING *');
        }
        return $query;
    }
```

Ok, so I can add

``` PHP
protected function _buildInsertPart($parts, $query, $generator) {
        $table = $parts[0];
        $columns = $this->_stringifyExpressions($parts[1], $generator);
        return sprintf('INSERT INTO %s (%s) OUTPUT Inserted.id', $table, implode(', ', $columns));
    }
```

to the `Cake\Database\SqlserverCompiler` but will the _returned id_ be saved to the original PHP entity object?
